This is a shot in the dark (I haven't used tapes in a long time) but I think
that you have to remove the "n" because it prevents the tape from being
rewound, i.e. do this instead:

mt -f /dev/rmt0h erase

(note that there is no "n" in "/dev/rmt0h") and see what happens.  If memory
serves, the "n" is used so that you can save multiple partitions to one tape in
sequential order.  If you omitted the "n", the tape would rewind each time
after saving a partition, overwriting data from the previous sequential save. 
Hope this helps.


