A raw device is normally required for swap space and the system assumes its 
the second, b, partition on the boot device. Apart from that partitioning is  
mainly a practical matter - exporting file systems, backing up and restoring 
are less of a hassle if only part of a disk is dealt with at any one time. 
Also, when you have to administer dozens of different machines in various 
clusters a similar, and familiar, disk/partition setup is an advantage.
My usual configuration for a system disk on a medium sized machines is as 
follows ..
a    	Root    	/    	    	250Mbs - /tmp is a link to /var/tmp
b    	swap    	    	    	512Mbs - at least 2.5 times memory
d    	System    	/usr     	2Gbs
e    	Var    	/var    	250Mbs - if not mail server

/usr/local and /home will normally be on another disk or disks. As would 
/var too if the machine was a mail server. If the sizes seem generous, 
remember that many changes take place over time and a forced re-partitioning 
of a system is a lengthy process with UFS. No doubt others will have valid 
reasons for other configurations and AdvFS is quite another matter.
-- 
__________ Jeff Wilson


