Adjusting things on the fly can be done without a AdvFS Utilities 
license. You basically only need the AdvFS Utilities license to have a 
cool graphical UI, and for some more advanced features like combining 
more than one disk into a single AdvFS volume.

For a standard installation, I'd have a 128MB "a" Partition for / (that 
one has to be on an extra partition to be bootable), then a "b" 
partition for swap (be sure to make it *at least* twice the sice of 
your RAM), and the rest in g for one big AdvFS domain.

Inside that domain which resides on g you can put filesets for /usr, 
/var and /tmp, and then you can use "chfsets" to dynamically adjust the 
"fileset quotas" which contral how much percent of that g partition 
each of the filesets may occupy at maximum.

 JH> I usually partition disks to allow for ease of backup - if 
 JH> have a tape device that takes
 JH> 9Gb - then I make 9Gb partitons, if I have a 20gb tape 
 JH> device then 20gb partitions.
 JH> Nothing more annoying than having to wait for backups that 
 JH> need a second tape to finish.

That's a good strategy IMO. In this case, just adjust the fileset 
quotas inside the "g" domain so that each fileset will take up your 
tape size at most, or maybe two combined at most the tape size.

