In article <20010531120832.12008.00000221@ng-fc1.aol.com>, spammerspitter@aol.com.invalid (SpitsOnSpammers) writes:

>Thanks to all for their replies.  I guess that since I have a 600au with 512MB
>RAM, I should err on the conservative side and make my swap partition 1280MB
>(2.5 times RAM size).

The size of the page/swap space has to be sufficient to support the amount
of virtual memory you plan to use.  If you actually plan to use that much
memory, expect the system to start paging at some point, which will adversely
affect the performance.  If you really do need this much virtual memory,
and have more than one disk, at least think about spreading the page/swap
over multiple disks.

>Currently, the default for the root partition is 340MB
>with the preinstalled Unix but I'm planning to wipe it out and reinstall from
>the Tru64 5.1 Binaries CDROM package I bought along with the 600au.  I was
>thinking of making the root partition double the size of vmunix.

The /vmunix on my V5.1 system is around 12 MB.  As written that says you
plan to make the root about 24 MB.  That's a bit too small...  If you
mean double what you're using now, that's probably more than enough
and will just waste space.  Kernel crash dumps won't get written to
the root, they go into /var/adm/crash.  If you're worried about a
crash filling up a file system, worry about that one.  The area likely
to full up on the root file system is /tmp.

>Might come in
>handy if I get a kernel panic and vmunix dumps core (had a situation on an
>Alphaserver once where the system kept coming up and going down like a yo-yo
>because the coredump couldn't be written to disk due to lack of space).  What
>does anybody think?

On the system disk, partitioned is the way the installation expects the
system to be.  As someone else noted, page/swap device have to be raw
partitions anyway.  For non-system disk file systems, you have more
options.  If you want to create separate partitions for individual
file systems take advantage of it.  Partitioning by itself doesn't
offer any performances benefits.  If you spread multiple AdvFS
domains across partitions on the same disk, the extra seeking to
keep the different logs consistent may hurt overall performance.

Having separate partitions for a single domain (multiple "volume")
isn't recommended, probably for reliability and performance reason,
through I've never seen any detail.

You can use partitioning to improve performance, but have to lose
some capacity to do so.  I've heard of the technique being used
at the hardware level and called "short-strokeing".  When you
reduce the usable capacity of a drive, the physical organization
of blocks will typically also limit the potential seek distance.
This can improve seek times.  Transfer rates on banded disks often
depend on the bands used, so you could partition the disk to make
the usable space have better seek characteristics and use the
higher transfer rate bands.  But, you pretty much have to give
up access to the extra storage.
  
