I asked the Compaq C++ team about this and got the following reply:

We consistently test our compilers on the Stepanov benchmark internally,
and have for at least the past two years been able to produce low
abstraction penalties in the range of 1.00 - 1.04.  However, to 
obtain this penalty, we require that you add two more options
-tused and -inline all.  -tused asks the compiler to add the template
instantiations into the object file (as opposed to creating them
in a repository which is the deault) and -inline all helps fine-tune
our internal inlining heuristics.

With these two additional options, this is what I see:

> a.out

test      absolute   additions      ratio with
number    time       per second     test0

 0        0.20sec    250.01M         1.00
 1        0.20sec    250.01M         1.00
 2        0.22sec    230.78M         1.08
 3        0.20sec    250.01M         1.00
 4        0.22sec    230.78M         1.08
 5        0.20sec    250.01M         1.00
 6        0.22sec    230.78M         1.08
 7        0.20sec    250.01M         1.00
 8        0.22sec    230.78M         1.08
 9        0.20sec    250.01M         1.00
10        0.22sec    230.78M         1.08
11        0.20sec    250.01M         1.00
12        0.22sec    230.78M         1.08
mean:     0.21sec    240.94M         1.04

Total absolute time: 2.70 sec

Abstraction Penalty: 1.04

We are working on not requiring the user to add the two
additonal options, but we do not have a firm deadline on
this goal.

-model ansi is a new object model which produces objects
incompatible with objects generated from older C++ versions
(The default object model, -model arm, has limitations in its
design.  The new object model will allow us to be fully
compliant with the ANSI/ISO C++ standard).  We suggest that 
you use the -model ansi option only if you can recompile all 
your sources and libraries.  We have NOT specifically added
any optimizations exclusively to the new object model, such
that it produces code faster than the older one.  However, note 
that the new object model was designed to be more efficient than
model arm.


