> >  -----  Posted via NewsOne.Net: Free (anonymous) Usenet News via the Web  -----
> >   http://newsone.net/ -- Free reading and anonymous posting to 60,000+ groups
> >    NewsOne.Net prohibits users from posting spam.  If this or other posts
> > made through NewsOne.Net violate posting guidelines, email abuse@newsone.net

Hi Andrew,

There are two limits for open files that you must be aware.

The first one that the process reaches is the soft limit
(open-max-soft), and you can change it on the fly typing "ulimit -n
new_limit", and new_limit could not exceed the hard limit
(open-max-hard). "ulimit -Ha" reports hard limits and "ulimit -a"
reports soft limits by default. Try ulimit(1) online manual page for
more detailed information.

If the process reaches the soft limit, the kernel sends an alarm to
the process, but it's not stopped. However, when the process reaches
the hard limit the kernel sends a SIGSTOP and the process returns
EMFILE error (errno 24).

The default value for both hard an soft limits is 4096, but if you
want to increase this values, you should use dxkerneltuner(8X) or
sysconfigdb(8) to do it and reboot the system.

Regards,
Pinho


