In article <3b13fcaa@nubby2.>, Aaron Banks <banks45@yahoo.com> wrote:

X  This may be simple.  I want to search for a certain text string in all files
X  on my system.  How do I do that?

write a simple script:

#!/bin/ksh
find starting_directory -type f -print | while read file
do
    if grep -q "search string" $file
    then
        echo $file
    fi
done

Modify the script as needed.  This script will start searching in
whatever directory you substitue for 'starting_directory', and will
grep each regular file looking for "search string" as specified by you. 
If it finds a file that includes the string, it will display the name
of the file where it was found.

                                        Bob Harris


