It is V5.0 when the device naming became independent of the a disk
SCSI bus address.

>
>I think I should have asked a more basic question and would appreciate
>further reassurance from a more knowledgeable source (ie, anyone :).
>
>
>I put the card in without doing anything else.  The machine won't boot.
>Well, actually, it will boot, repetively, and not stop.  It gets
>past recognizing and mounting the internal disks and then starts
>over.

For what it is worth, "starting over" is a bit weak as problem
descriptions go.  Does the system panic?  Simply stop and reboot?
Error message just before it starts to reboot would be useful.

It does sound like you've missed a step in the installation
process.  Since the KZPCA is the first of its type, the original
kernel probably doesn't have a driver for it.  The lack of driver
may be causing the problem.  Try booting the generic kernel
(/genvmunix) and build a new kernel.  If the lack of driver
is the problem, then the generic kernel should allow booting
to the point that a new kernel can be built.  You may also
want to check the SPD for the version of the operating system
you're running and the QuickSpecs for the system to verify that
the particular SCSI adapter is supported.

If you haven't built a kernel that can recognize the adapter,
I don't trying to answer the remaining questions is useful,
since they're probably not relevant.

The System Management guide should have instructions for
building a new kernel.  If you don't have a printed copy,
there will be one on the Documentation CDROM.

