Michael Reffalt wrote:
> 
> I have a user who needs to launch a flow dynamics
> problem and is running into the data ceiling when he executes.
> Other than temp. reseting with ulimit -d how can I set the data
> limit higher.  Below are my current defaults
> 
> # /usr/bin/ulimit -a
> time(seconds)        unlimited
> file(blocks)         unlimited
> data(kbytes)         200000
> stack(kbytes)        2048
> memory(kbytes)       1022336
> coredump(blocks)     unlimited
> nofiles(descriptors) 4096
> vmemory(kbytes)      1048576
> 
> Thanks for the help!

You need to put some entries in /etc/sysconfigtab which is read at boot
time. My entry has the following, adapt as required:

proc:
        max-per-proc-address-space = 2147483648
        per-proc-address-space = 2147483648
        max-per-proc-data-size = 2147483648
        per-proc-data-size = 262144000
        max-per-proc-stack-size = 268435456

see the man page entries for sysconfig, sysconfigtab and sysconfigdb for
guidence.        



