In article <9ebo2i$9gd$1@news01.cit.cornell.edu>, klgthree@spam.cornell.edu () writes:
>
>I would like to add a sn-kzpca-aa (LVD controller) scsi controller
>to an xp1000 667 running tru64 5.1.  This would be used to run
>an external tape drive only.
>
>An installation document that came with the board gives an
>ominous message--"It is NOT recommended that this controller be installed on
>an existing Tru64 UNIX system disk due to the difficulty
>in reassigning the system disk to a new controller and getting the
>partitions right on the system disk."

In V4 and earlier (when that document was probably written), the
name of a disk was dependent on its bus address (bus #, target #
and logical unit number).  The bus number was dependent on where
the board was installed and when that slot was configured by the
kernel.  Sometimes a new board would be configured before the
one previously installed and get that board's bus number, thus
changing all the device names.

In V5, device names for most devices are assigned as the devices
are first discovered; the first disk becoming dsk0, the 2nd dsk1
and so on.  The serial number of the drive was used to track where
it was installed, so that no matter how the bus address changed,
the same special file could be associated with the same disk.
For suitable devices, the problem described by that installation
guide simply went away.

