Brian Cuttler wrote:
> 
> Ok, assuming that I fowled up tuning my system...
> 
> On VMS I'd boot sysconfig and correct the parameters, on True64
> I'm a bit less certain of the analog procedure.
> 
> Also, since this is a loaner/demo system I lack the documentation.
> 
> If someone could be so kind, I need a fairly detailed procedure, not
> at base 0, I can figure out "boot installation disk" (assuming they
> sent us one, it was a pre-installed OS), just no big gaps in the
> procedure.
> 

I'm assuming you mean that the boot fails as a result of some tuning
param you entered.

If you boot the installation CD then you need to mount the the real root
filesystem from the hard disk. If this is a UFS filesystem then you can
just mount the partition on /mnt. If the root is an Advfs then you need
to create the directory entries in /etc/fdmns as follows having first
identified which disk it is you need to mount!

1) Create a directory /etc/fdmns/recovery

2) Create a symbolic link in the above directory to the disk device for
the root partition. If it is a V4.0 system then something like ln -s
/dev/rz0a rz0a. For a V5 system then it would be ln -s dsk0a
/dev/disk/dsk0a. Adjust disk name accordingly for you system.

3) Mount the filesystem:

  mount recovery#root  /mnt

4) edit /mnt/etc/sysconfigtab as required.

5) sync

6) shutdown and reboot.

Alternatively you could try an interactive boot and reset the parameters
for that boot. This should get you in so you can correct the entries in
sysconfigtab. To do this boot with flags "ia" ie

>>> boot -fl "ia"

This should start booting and at some point stop to ask for your kernel
name. At this point you should be able to give the name and enter a new
value for a tuning param. ie

vmunix proc:maxusers=2048

This means boot using vmunix and set the maxusers param in the proc
subsystem to 2048


Have fun!

