Eric Bennett wrote:
> 
> I have some machines behind a masquerading firewall, and I want them to be
> able to NFS-mount machines on the other (public) side of the firewall (NOT
> the reverse, I am NOT trying to have machines on the public side of the
> firewall access an NFS server on the private side).  I can get this to work
> manually if I use TCP-based NFS:
> mount -o proto=tcp host:/filesystem /localmountpoint
> 
> I would prefer to use the automounter to do this

automount requires "end to end" authentication.  In other words both the
forward (name to address) and reverse (address to name) lookups have to
match.  You may be able to trick this into working by adding the names
and address of each machine in each /etc/hosts and then make certain
that /etc/svc.conf looks up hosts in "files" first.


Jack Patteeuw


